# pdfpc settings for this talk (pdfpc 4.5 or later): copy this file to
# ~/.config/pdfpc/pdfpcrc. With pdfpc 4.7 you can instead pass each option line
# with -c, e.g. -c "option prerender-slides 30".
#
# pdfpc draws a moving page up to four times (projector, current, next, notes) and
# starts the page's time only once it is on screen. Drawn live, a 45 ms frame took
# 330 ms. With these settings it draws the next move while you talk at a stop and
# plays it from memory: presenter.pdf's 34 ms frames take 40-49 ms, the moves' design
# (measured 2026-10-07, pdfpc 4.7, a 3440x1440 projector). Before the talk press
# End, Home, Ctrl+T: pdfpc leaves the notes view undrawn until a page changes.
#
# the next move (at most 25 pages) and the stop it lands on, drawn ahead
option prerender-slides 30
# starting 1 s after you arrive at a stop (ready about 4 s later)
option prerender-delay 1
# and kept as drawn: unpacking a compressed page costs 20-30 ms at projector size.
# Short of memory? Delete this line: pages are then kept PNG-packed (about 0.5 MB
# each instead of 14-20 MB) and the moves play smoothly but somewhat slower.
option cache-max-usize 100000
# for 5 minutes: about 20 MB a page at 1440p (14 MB at 1080p), 2-3 GB at a talk's pace;
# clicking fast through a rehearsal holds more (3.8 GB after 8 moves in 2.5 minutes)
option cache-expiration 300
